/** The guide's Markdown fragments, by file name without `.md`. */
export const FRAGMENTS: Record<string, string> = {
  "booking": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Ordering\n\n{{/if}}\n- Call `{{tool:hotels book}}` only for the offer the user picked, with at least one guest per room{{#if claude-skill cli}}, named as the user gave them{{/if}}; never invent guests.{{#if claude-skill cli}} Room 1 is the offer's first room, and a room takes no more guests than it sleeps.{{/if}}\n- Guest names: Latin letters as on the passport (Müller → Mueller), first and last name 1–{{limit:guestName}} characters each{{#if cli}}, as `--guest 1:John:Smith` (room 1, first name, last name){{/if}}.\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n- The contact's first and last name (up to {{limit:contactFirstName}} characters each), email and phone (with its country code) are all required. Special requests must be in English and aren't guaranteed.\n{{/if}}\n- `{{tool:hotels book}}` can take up to about two minutes{{#if cli}}: allow the command at least 3 minutes{{/if}}. If it times out, {{#if cli}}run it again with identical flags{{/if}}{{#if mcp claude-skill}}call it again with the same details{{/if}}: it returns the same order.\n{{#if mcp}}\n- On `offer_changed`, show the user every change and ask again before ordering the new offer.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if cli}}\n- `{{tool:hotels book}}` takes only these fields: a `--body` with any other field (such as `nationality`) is rejected with exit 2.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "errors": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Errors\n\n{{#if claude-skill}}A tool error{{/if}}{{#if cli}}An error on stderr{{/if}} carries `code`, `message` and `retryable`, and `retryAfterSeconds` when waiting helps; many carry `details`. Search errors are under Search. Ordering errors:\n\n| `code` | Do this |\n|---|---|\n| `offer_changed` (409) | The price or terms changed since the offer: `changes` lists every change (`worse` says which are worse for the user) and `offer` is the new offer. Show the user every change and ask again; if they still want it, order the new offer, and confirm the new order before giving its link |\n| `offer_expired` (409) | Get offers again and let the user choose again |\n| `offer_unavailable` (422) | The offer is sold out or was refused: get offers again and let the user choose again |\n| `order_in_progress` (409) | The same order is still being created: wait `retryAfterSeconds`, then {{#if claude-skill}}call again with the same details{{/if}}{{#if cli}}run the command again with identical flags{{/if}} |\n| `offer_already_ordered` (409) | This offer was already ordered with other details: show that order (`orderId`) instead. An offer is ordered once |\n| `order_exists` (409) | This stay already has an order (`orderId`, `status`): show it instead of ordering again. This also blocks ordering the same offer, dates and rooms again for another party, ordering a stay again after it was cancelled, ordering while the user's own website checkout for the stay is open (up to 3 hours 45 minutes) and ordering again a stay whose earlier order failed at the hotel. For those, the user emails {{support}} |\n| `order_attempts_exhausted` (409) | This order can't be tried again: the user emails {{support}} with its `reference` |\n| `order_removed` (410) | The order was never completed and no payment is held: get offers again and order the new offer (the order keeps its `orderId`) |\n| `stay_unsupported` (422) | `mixed_occupancy`: order once per party; `max_nights`: at most `max` nights per order |\n| `stay_in_past` (422) | Check-in has passed in the hotel's time zone: ask for new dates |\n| `ordering_paused` (503) | Ordering is paused on Fortrip's side: tell the user, and try again only after `retryAfterSeconds` (10 minutes or more) |\n\nAny code:\n\n- `invalid_request` (400): `details.issues` lists each bad field; fix them. For amenities, `details.filterable` lists those that can filter.\n- `cursor_invalid` (400): the page is gone; search again from the first page.\n- `not_found` (404): no such hotel, or no such order for this user.\n- `rate_limited` (429): wait `retryAfterSeconds`, then retry once.\n- `service_unavailable` (503) and `supplier_unavailable` (503 or 504): wait `retryAfterSeconds`, then retry once with the same details; if it fails again, tell the user.\n- Any other code: read `message`. If `retryable` is true, wait `retryAfterSeconds` and retry once; otherwise tell the user instead of guessing.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "meals": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Presenting an offer\n\nGive the user, for each offer:\n\n- the room (`room.name`, beds, size) and the total, as above;\n- the meals: `meals.included` (breakfast, lunch, dinner) or `allInclusive`, with `guestsCoveredPerRoom`. Fewer than the guests per room means not everyone gets the meals (breakfast for 1 with 2 adults in the room): say who is covered. `unspecifiedMainMeals` counts main meals the hotel didn't name. With `known: false`, the meals are unknown: say so, and don't call it room only;\n- cancellation: `status` (`free`, `partial_penalty`, `non_refundable` or `unknown`), `freeCancellationEndsAt` (null with `free`: free until check-in) and the `penalties`. Times are in UTC: also give them in the hotel's `timeZone`;\n- confirmation: `mode` `instant` or `on_request` (within `withinMinutes`);\n- fees payable at the property (`payAtProperty.items`), in the property's currency. `disclosure: \"incomplete\"` means there can be fees not listed: say so.\n\nAn offer expires at `expiresAt`, about 30 minutes after it was made; after that, get offers again.\n\n\"Not listed\" is not \"no\": `listed: false` (amenities, policies, rooms), `null` and `unknown` mean the source didn't say. Tell the user it's unknown instead of turning it into \"no\".\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp}}\n- \"Not listed\" or \"unknown\" is not \"no\".\n{{/if}}\n",
  "occupancy": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Rooms and guests\n\n- {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`stay.rooms` lists each room's party as `{ adults, childrenAges }`: two rooms of 2 adults, one with a child of 6, is `[{ adults: 2, childrenAges: [6] }, { adults: 2, childrenAges: [] }]`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--room <adults[:age,…]>`, once per room, gives each room's party: two rooms of 2 adults, one with a child of 6, is `--room 2:6 --room 2`{{/if}}.\n- Ask each child's age; offers need it.\n- If the party doesn't split evenly across the rooms (3 adults in 2 rooms), ask the user how it splits.\n{{/if}}\n- Offers and orders take one party per call, every room the same: for different parties, order once per party.{{#if claude-skill cli}} `{{tool:hotels offers}}` answers `stay_unsupported` (`mixed_occupancy`) for mixed rooms; `{{tool:hotels search}}` accepts them but prices only from the dates, with the note `party_split_required`.{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n- Limits: 1–{{limit:rooms}} rooms, 1–{{limit:adultsPerRoom}} adults and 0–{{limit:childrenPerRoom}} children (aged 0–{{limit:childAgeMax}}) per room, 1–{{limit:nightsSupplier}} nights, up to {{limit:offersHotels}} hotels per `{{tool:hotels offers}}` call. A longer stay answers `stay_unsupported` (`max_nights`): ask the user whether to split it into consecutive stays, each its own order.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "order": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Workflow\n\n1. **Search** with `{{tool:hotels search}}` for candidate hotels: a destination, `{{arg:text}}` or both, and the stay's dates and rooms for indicative prices.\n{{#if cli}}\n\n   ```bash\n   {{tool:hotels search}} --place-name Bangkok --country TH --check-in 2026-11-10 \\\n     --check-out 2026-11-12 --room 2 --stars-min 4 --sort price_asc --limit 10\n   ```\n\n{{/if}}\n2. **Details** (optional) with `{{tool:hotels get}}`, when the user asks about a hotel or a stated preference needs evidence (a pool, a policy, room size). For a details-only question, call it directly when you know the hotel's `hotelId`; otherwise find the hotel with `{{tool:hotels search}}`.\n{{#if cli}}\n\n   ```bash\n   {{tool:hotels get}} <hotelId> [--rooms]\n   ```\n\n{{/if}}\n3. **Offers** with `{{tool:hotels offers}}` for a few shortlisted hotels, with the dates and rooms the user will order. It takes about 15 seconds. A hotel is available only once this returns an offer for it.\n{{#if cli}}\n\n   ```bash\n   {{tool:hotels offers}} --hotel <hotelId> --hotel <hotelId> --check-in 2026-11-10 \\\n     --check-out 2026-11-12 --room 2 --room 2 [--require-free-cancellation]\n   ```\n\n{{/if}}\n4. **Order** with `{{tool:hotels book}}`, once the user has picked one offer and given the guests and the contact (see Ordering).\n{{#if cli}}\n\n   ```bash\n   {{tool:hotels book}} --offer <offerId> --guest 1:John:Smith --guest 2:Mary:Smith \\\n     --first-name John --last-name Smith --email john@example.com \\\n     --phone \"+8613800000000\"\n   ```\n\n{{/if}}\n5. **Confirm, then give the payment link**, as in \"Before giving the payment link\" below.\n6. **After payment**, check the order with `{{tool:orders get}}` (see After payment).\n\n## Search\n\n### Where\n\n- **A place by name:** {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ kind: \"place_name\", name, countryCode }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--place-name <name> --country <CC>`{{/if}}, in any language (Kyoto or 京都). The country is required: ask the user when it isn't clear. Names match exactly, ignoring only case and spacing: accents, hyphens, apostrophes and periods count (\"Köln\" or \"Cologne\", not \"Koln\"; \"St. Moritz\", not \"St Moritz\").\n- If several places bear the name, the answer is `destination_ambiguous` with up to {{limit:candidatesShown}} candidates and their hotel counts: ask the user which one they mean, then search it by its `placeId`; never pick one yourself.\n- `destination_not_found` with `no_match` means no place of that name in that country: check the spelling and accents, or try another name for the place. Its `nameAsText` counts the hotels that a `{{arg:text}}` search for the name would find.\n- **A place from an earlier answer:** {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ kind: \"place\", placeId }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--place <placeId>`{{/if}}.\n- **Near a point:** {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ kind: \"point\", location: { lat, lng } }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--point <lat,lng>`{{/if}}, for \"near X\" when you know where X is.\n- **A whole country:** {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ kind: \"country\", countryCode }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--country-only <CC>`{{/if}}, as for the Maldives.\n- Countries, regions (states, provinces), prefecture-level cities in China and main islands cover their whole area. Towns, neighbourhoods, districts, small islands and points cover a circle of {{limit:radiusMetersDefault}} meters; `{{arg:destination.radiusMeters}}` sets another, up to {{limit:radiusMetersMax}} meters, and narrows an area to a circle around its center.\n- **Localities:** an area search lists its `localities`, the towns its hotels belong to, each with a `placeId`, its hotel `count` and the places it `includes` (Patong is part of Phuket). To search one, pass it as {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ kind: \"locality\", placeId }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--locality <placeId>`{{/if}}. A town that isn't a locality answers `destination_not_found` with `not_a_locality` and the `hub` it belongs to: search the hub, or the town itself with {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`kind: \"place\"`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--place`{{/if}} for a circle around it.\n- A price sort or price filter over more than {{limit:poolLimit}} hotels (in an area, or for text alone) answers `search_too_broad` with `matched` and the top `localities`: show them and ask the user which they prefer, unless the request already names one of them; search that one by its `placeId` as a locality, never by its name.\n\n### Text\n\n`{{arg:text}}` matches exact words in hotel names and addresses, in Chinese or English:\n\n- Every word must match as written: plurals, accents, spacing and abbreviations count (\"hostel\" and \"hostels\", \"St Regis\" and \"Saint Regis\" find different hotels). Hyphens and apostrophes don't (Ritz-Carlton finds Ritz Carlton).\n- In Chinese, matching follows word segmentation, so part of a name can match unrelated hotels (希尔 is not 希尔顿).\n- Use only distinctive words: a hotel or brand name, a district or a landmark. Not \"hotel\", \"near\", \"cheap\" or \"best\".\n- Typos return nothing: \"Shangahi\" finds no hotel.\n- Place names belong in the destination: \"Maldives\" as text finds a hotel named Maldives in China.\n\n### Filters\n\n- Stars run from 1 to 5 in half steps{{#if mcp claude-skill}} (`filters.stars`){{/if}}. \"4 stars\" as {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ min: 4, max: 4 }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--stars-min 4 --stars-max 4`{{/if}} leaves out 4.5: unless the user means exactly 4, use {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ min: 4 }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--stars-min 4`{{/if}} or ask. Unrated hotels are left out whenever stars are filtered.\n- `{{arg:filters.amenities}}` takes only the amenities that can filter (such as pool, parking, wifi); others, such as gym or spa, show in `{{tool:hotels get}}` but can't filter. A hotel matches only when it lists the amenity, and about half the hotels list no amenities at all (`coverage.excludedNoFacilityData` counts those left out): \"not listed\" is not \"absent\".\n- `{{arg:filters.price.maxPerRoomNight}}` bounds the indicative price per room per night, in USD; hotels without one are left out and counted.\n\n### Results\n\n- `notes` explain the answer (a limited pool, a circle around an island, more hotels just outside it): pass on what matters to the user.\n- `coverage` has the counts: `matched` hotels, how many were priced and how many have a price.\n- A next page is {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`cursor` set to `nextCursor` and, optionally, `limit`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--cursor <nextCursor>` and, optionally, `--limit`{{/if}}; nothing else.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp}}\n- A place name needs its `countryCode` (ask the user if unclear) and matches exactly (accents and periods count). On `destination_ambiguous`, ask the user which they mean, then search its `placeId`; never pick one yourself.\n- On `search_too_broad`, show its `localities` and ask the user which, unless they already named one; search it as `{ kind: \"locality\", placeId }`, never by name.\n- `text` matches whole words of hotel names and addresses: use a distinctive name, brand or landmark. A typo finds nothing.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "payment-link": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Before giving the payment link\n\n`{{tool:hotels book}}` answers with an unpaid order: `status` `awaiting_payment`, its `reference` (the support number), `price.total`, the stay and its terms, and `payment.url`, a Fortrip page that recaps the order and continues to payment on Stripe. Nothing is charged until the user pays.\n\n1. Show the order's total and currency, the stay (hotel, room, dates, rooms and guests per room) and every term: meals, cancellation (status, deadline, penalties) and fees payable at the property, unknowns included.\n2. End that reply by asking the user to confirm, and wait for a clear yes. Urgency is not consent.\n3. Then give `payment.url` exactly as returned: don't shorten, rebuild or re-encode it.\n\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp}}\n- Before you give `payment.url`, show the user the order's total and currency, the stay and every term, end that reply by asking the user to confirm, and wait for a clear yes. Then give `payment.url` exactly as returned, to this user only: anyone holding it can pay.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n- `payment.url` works like a payment credential: anyone holding it can pay. Give it to this user only, never in shared channels, documents, logs or tickets. With `requiresSignIn: true` (an order started on the Fortrip website), the user signs in to pay.\n- The link expires at `payment.expiresAt`; the order is then `expired`. To order the stay again, get offers again, and confirm the new order with the user before giving its link.\n- An order `awaiting_payment` whose `payment.url` is null had its link unreadable this time: read it again with `{{tool:orders get}}`.\n{{/if}}\n- Never ask for, accept or pass on payment card details; payment happens only on the payment page.{{#if claude-skill cli}} A card number in a guest name, the contact or the special requests is rejected{{#if cli}} with exit 2{{/if}}.{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n\n## After payment\n\nCheck the order with `{{tool:orders get}}`:\n\n- `confirming` while the hotel confirms, then `confirmed` with the hotel's `confirmationNumber`.\n- `failed`: no stay was booked; `payment.status` says whether the payment was released or refunded.\n- `needs_attention`: Fortrip is looking into it; the user can email {{support}} with the `reference`.\n- `manageUrl` is the user's own page for the order.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "planning": "# Planning a hotel stay\n\nRead this when the request is more than one hotel for fixed dates: flexible dates, a budget, split stays, several destinations, or \"find me the best\". The rules in {{#if mcp}}the server instructions{{/if}}{{#if claude-skill}}SKILL.md{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`{{tool:guide}}`{{/if}} still apply (call order, destinations, one party per offer and order, confirming the order `{{tool:hotels book}}` returns before giving the payment link); this covers what to ask, how to compare and how to present.{{#if cli}} As there, `fortrip` is short for `npx -y fortrip@latest`.{{/if}}\n\n## What to collect\n\nKeep track of: dates (or the flexible window), nights, destination(s) with their country, rooms and the guests in each room, room constraints (beds, connecting rooms, accessibility), budget, and what the user said matters most (cheapest, most flexible, closest to X).\n\nOffers and orders take one party per call, every room the same, so if the party doesn't split evenly across the rooms, ask how it splits. Rooms for different parties are priced and ordered separately, each order with its own payment link.\n\nAsk only for what changes which hotels qualify or how they rank. \"4 stars\" as {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ min: 4, max: 4 }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--stars-min 4 --stars-max 4`{{/if}} leaves out 4.5: unless the user means exactly 4, use {{#if mcp claude-skill}}`{ min: 4 }`{{/if}}{{#if cli}}`--stars-min 4`{{/if}} or ask. If children come along, ask each child's age; `{{tool:hotels offers}}` needs it. Don't ask for trip type or style: infer it from the conversation or leave it general. When a place name is ambiguous, ask which place the user means.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- A `{{tool:hotels search}}` price is an indicative estimate per room per night, not a quote. Never call a search result available or bookable, and once you have offers, compare offers only. A hotel without a search price can still have offers.\n- Use `{{tool:hotels get}}` only when a stated preference needs evidence (a pool, a policy, room size). An amenity or policy it doesn't list is unknown, not absent.\n- A hotel is available only once `{{tool:hotels offers}}` returned an offer for it.\n- Anything {{#if mcp claude-skill}}the tools{{/if}}{{#if cli}}the CLI{{/if}} didn't return, such as reviews, style, price trends or exchange rates other than `approx`, is unknown. Say so instead of filling it in.\n\n## Ranking\n\n1. Drop options that break a hard constraint (dates, guests, budget cap, required facility). Compare a budget in the unit the user gave it: per night, per room or for the whole stay. An offer's `price.total` covers the whole stay and every room, in USD.\n2. Keep only options with a live offer.\n3. Order by the user's stated main goal. Goals they didn't state don't count.\n4. Use other preferences only to break ties.\n\nUse the cheapest comparable offer as the baseline unless the user fixed the room type. Show at most three plans, each with its price difference from the baseline and what it trades off (meals, cancellation, location, confirmation). Keep hotel names as returned and dates in the user's notation.\n\nFor a split stay or several destinations, price each leg with its own `{{tool:hotels offers}}` call and add up the legs. Each leg is a separate `{{tool:hotels book}}` call, with its own confirmation and its own payment link.\n\n## Stop and say so\n\n- `{{tool:hotels offers}}` covers up to {{limit:offersHotels}} hotels per call and takes about 15 seconds, and its offers expire after about 30 minutes. If the request needs more hotels or date combinations than a few calls can cover, narrow it with the user, or present the result as a comparison of the hotels you priced, not the best overall.\n- When a search is too broad to price, show its localities and ask the user which they prefer, unless the request already names one of them; never pick one yourself.\n- Urgency is not consent. Call `{{tool:hotels book}}` only for the offer the user picked, and give its payment link only after the user confirms the total, currency, stay and terms of the order `{{tool:hotels book}}` returned.\n",
  "prices": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Prices and currency\n\n- A search price (`price.fromPerRoomNight`) is indicative and not bookable: the lowest price of one unit (a room or, at a hostel, possibly a bed) per night, for any party and often any date, and it can be well above or below the offers. Call it an estimate, quote `{{tool:hotels offers}}` prices, and never call a search result available or bookable. `price: null` means there is no indicative price, not that the hotel is full; about half the hotels have none for given dates.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp}}\n- Search prices are indicative, not bookable: quote `{{tool:hotels offers}}` prices.\n{{/if}}\n- {{#if claude-skill cli}}In an offer or order, {{/if}}`price.total` is the whole stay for all rooms and is what is charged, in USD for offers and the orders made from them.{{#if claude-skill cli}} `perNightAllRooms` is the total per night; to compare with a per-room budget, also divide by the rooms.{{/if}} Stripe's page may let the user pay in their card's local currency at Stripe's rate (Adaptive Pricing){{#if claude-skill cli}}: say so plainly, and that the USD total is the price{{/if}}. `approx`{{#if claude-skill cli}}, in `{{arg:displayCurrency}}`, with its `rateDate`,{{/if}} is an estimate, never the charge.\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n- `{{arg:displayCurrency}}`{{#if cli}} (or `FORTRIP_DISPLAY_CURRENCY`){{/if}} is `USD` or `CNY`: `CNY` adds `approx` in yuan to suit a user who thinks in it. It never changes the charge.\n- Fees payable at the property (`payAtProperty`) are separate, in the property's own currency, not converted.\n- After payment, `payment.localCharge` is what the card paid in its own currency. Orders made on the Fortrip website can be charged in another currency: `payment.chargeCurrency` says which.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "scope": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n# Fortrip hotel stays\n\n{{/if}}\nFortrip: hotel search, live offers and orders with a payment link.{{#if claude-skill cli}} Not for flights, activities, or changing or cancelling an existing order.{{/if}}\n\n{{#if mcp}}\nCall order: `{{tool:hotels search}}` → (optional) `{{tool:hotels get}}` → `{{tool:hotels offers}}` → `{{tool:hotels book}}`; after the user pays, `{{tool:orders get}}`; `{{tool:orders list}}` lists their orders. `{{tool:guide}}` returns Fortrip's guidance for flexible dates, budgets or several destinations.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill}}\nThe tools are on Fortrip's MCP server, named `fortrip` in this plugin (a Fortrip connector added on claude.ai can show under its own name). They're written `fortrip:<tool>` below:\n{{/if}}\n{{#if cli}}\nThe `fortrip` CLI (npm package `fortrip`) calls Fortrip as the signed-in user and prints each answer as compact text (`--json` prints its JSON):\n{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n\n| {{#if claude-skill}}Tool{{/if}}{{#if cli}}Command{{/if}} | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `{{tool:hotels search}}` | Hotels in a place, near a point or matching a name (`{{arg:text}}`), with indicative prices |\n| `{{tool:hotels get}}` | One hotel's address, check-in and check-out times, description, policies, amenities and rooms; no prices |\n| `{{tool:hotels offers}}` | Live offers (room, meals, total in USD, cancellation terms) for up to {{limit:offersHotels}} hotels |\n| `{{tool:hotels book}}` | An unpaid order for one offer, with its terms and a payment link |\n| `{{tool:orders get}}` | One order and its status, including whether the hotel confirmed it |\n| `{{tool:orders list}}` | The user's orders, newest first, including those made on the Fortrip website |\n\nFor flexible dates, a budget, split stays, several destinations or \"find me the best\", {{#if claude-skill}}read [references/planning.md](references/planning.md){{/if}}{{#if cli}}also run `{{tool:guide}} planning`{{/if}} before searching.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "setup": "{{#if cli}}\n## Setup\n\nRun every command as `npx -y fortrip@latest <command>` (Node.js 22 or newer). The examples below shorten that to `fortrip`; `fortrip <command> --help` lists a command's flags. `{{tool:hotels search}}`, `{{tool:hotels offers}}` and `{{tool:hotels book}}` also take `--body '<json>'` (or `--body @file.json`), the raw request body; flags override its fields. Add `--json` to any command that calls Fortrip to get its answer as one line of JSON instead of text.\n\nWhen the user first asks about hotels, run `{{tool:whoami}}` before asking follow-up questions, so the sign-in can happen while they answer.\n\n{{/if}}\n{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Sign-in\n\n{{/if}}\nSign-in happens only in the browser. Never ask the user to paste a token.\n{{#if claude-skill}}\n\nIf none of these tools are available, Fortrip isn't connected: tell the user to add the Fortrip connector or plugin and sign in.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp claude-skill}}\nIf a tool says the user isn't signed in, ask them to reconnect Fortrip.\n{{/if}}\n{{#if cli}}\n\nIf a command exits with code **3** and stderr has `loginUrl`, show it to the user as a link together with `userCode`, e.g. \"Sign in to Fortrip: <loginUrl> (code <userCode>), then tell me when you're done.\" When they confirm, rerun the same command; it finishes the sign-in itself. Don't run `{{tool:login}}`: it waits for up to 10 minutes. Commands that exit 3 together share one `loginUrl`; show it once. If the rerun gives a different `loginUrl`, show that one.\n\nWhen Fortrip no longer accepts the saved sign-in (it was revoked, or it expired and couldn't be renewed), the command also exits 3 with a `loginUrl`; handle it the same way. The saved sign-in is kept until the user approves the new link.\n\nIn CI or a sandbox without a browser, the user can set `FORTRIP_TOKEN` instead. Exit 3 with `tokenSource: \"FORTRIP_TOKEN\"` means Fortrip didn't accept that token: tell the user to replace or unset it.\n\n## Exit codes\n\n| Code | Meaning | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Success; the answer on stdout | Continue |\n| 1 | Any other error: an error from Fortrip (see Errors), a network error or a timeout | Read the error on stderr. If `retryable` is true, wait `retryAfterSeconds` (or a few seconds) and retry once |\n| 2 | Invalid arguments, or `invalid_request` from Fortrip | Fix the flags; the error lists each bad field with a message |\n| 3 | Not signed in | Show `loginUrl` to the user, then rerun (above) |\n| 4 | Rate limited | Wait `retryAfterSeconds`, then retry once |\n\nOutput on stdout or stderr can carry `warning` (with `recoveryFile` when a sign-in couldn't be saved): pass it on to the user.\n{{/if}}\n",
  "support": "{{#if claude-skill cli}}\n## Changes and support\n\n{{/if}}\n{{#if mcp claude-skill}}These tools{{/if}}{{#if cli}}The CLI{{/if}} can't change or cancel an order: for changes, cancellations, refunds or other problems, the user emails {{support}} with the order's `reference` (never `payment.url`).\n"
};
